1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A transport layer has two jobs to do. UDP does both and stops there.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Multiplexing is the port number. Integrity is the checksum.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Everything else is the application’s problem, by design.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
A postcard, not a registered letter.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Four fields, two octets each, so eight octets in all.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Source Port is optional: if it is not used, a zero goes there.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Length counts the header as well as the data.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Destination Port is the one field a router never rewrites.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Sixteen bits hold 65535, so no UDP datagram can be longer.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
So the largest payload is 65535 minus 8, which is 65527.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
IPv4 caps the total at 65535 too, so the payload drops to 65507.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
A DNS query is 33 octets, so its Length field says 41.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
The checksum also covers six words that are never transmitted.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Twelve octets: two addresses, a zero, protocol 17, and the length.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Including the addresses is what detects a misrouted datagram.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses, so its pseudo-header is 40 octets.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Step one: pad the data with zero octets to an even count.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Step two: add every 16-bit word as an unsigned number.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Step three: fold the carries back in, until 16 bits remain.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
If the result is zero, send FFFF. All zeros means no checksum at all.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
One datagram: 192.168.1.10 to 8.8.8.8, port 51515 to port 53.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Fifteen words in all: six pseudo, four header, five data.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The sum is B592, so the checksum is its complement: 4A6D.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Add it back in and the receiver gets FFFF. That is the test.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
A UDP socket is named by two things: destination address and port.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Three datagrams, two senders, one port: all three reach one socket.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
The same three flows over TCP would need two sockets.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Two datagrams to the same port are two separate messages.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Sixteen bits of port: 65536 values, 0 to 65535.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
The numbers worth memorising: 53, 67, 68, 69, 123, 161.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
One query out, one response back, and no connection at all.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
That is the trade: 8 octets of header instead of 20, and no safety net.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
No retransmission, no ordering, no flow control, no congestion control.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Over IPv6 the checksum is not optional, and zero is discarded.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
The header sits inside IP, so fragmentation counts it.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Seven fragments, the last carrying 8 octets, at offset 1110.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Question one: which length does the question mean?

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Question two: is the checksum being computed or verified?

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Question three: which pseudo-header, and which protocol number?

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Question four: how many sockets, and how many messages?

